/* Start/Stop: filled accent (affirmative actions), same primary treatment used for bottom
 * dialog/panel buttons elsewhere (see abstractdialog.qss) -- hover darkens/brightens the
 * background too, not just the text, kept in sync with that file.
 *
 * The :disabled rule matters more than usual here: the two buttons are mutually exclusive
 * (see qrcodescanner.cpp's cameraStarted/cameraStopped handling), so one of them is always
 * disabled -- same situation as the Video settings node's own Start/Stop pair.
 *
 * Vertical padding bumped from the structural 2px to 6px (light theme only, horizontal
 * padding unchanged at 12px) -- the same 6px 12px the dialog bottom buttons use
 * (abstractdialog.qss), and close to the Browse button's 4px 16px (subjectinfo.qss). */
uise--QrCodeScanner #buttonsFrame uise--PushButton QPushButton
{background-color: #218DAE; color: #EEEEEE; border: none; padding: 6px 12px;}

/* border:none is NOT redundant with the rule above. framewithmodalstatus.qss has
 * "uise--FrameWithModalStatus #buttonsFrame uise--PushButton QPushButton:hover
 * {border: 1px solid #444444;}" for the modal-status frame's own button row -- and since
 * QrCodeScanner's button row happens to use the same #buttonsFrame object name, and
 * AddContact/AddAccount wrap their content in a FrameWithModalStatus, that rule also matches
 * these buttons. Being :hover-qualified it outranks the non-hover border:none above, so
 * without restating border:none here a dark border appears on hover over the solid fill. */
uise--QrCodeScanner #buttonsFrame uise--PushButton QPushButton:hover
{color: #FFFFFF; background-color: #0099b8; border: none;}

uise--QrCodeScanner #buttonsFrame uise--PushButton QPushButton:disabled,
uise--QrCodeScanner #buttonsFrame uise--PushButton QPushButton:disabled:hover
{background-color: #DDDDDD; color: #999999; border: none;}
